        dek: "Augustine said we were made for something the world cannot give. The hunger is not weakness — it is a compass. But is that philosophy or apologetics?",
        objective: "The student can state Augustine's cor inquietum claim, distinguish evidence from proof, and evaluate one objection from non-belief.",
        parentBriefing: "This is intellectual history, not catechism. Augustine's argument: human beings want something they cannot name, and nothing they get satisfies permanently. He called that God. A secular student can engage the phenomenology (the restlessness is real) without accepting the theology (the restlessness points at God). Teach both layers. The discipline is honesty about the difference.",
        hardEdges: ["Augustine is not arguing 'believe because you're hungry.' He is arguing that the hunger is evidence — not proof. The distinction matters.", "This is a Christian claim about a Christian God. It is also a phenomenological claim about human experience. Teach both. Don't pretend it's only one."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The hunger as compass", body: "Augustine's Confessions opens with one of the most quoted lines in Western thought: 'You have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.' The claim is not 'God exists because you feel empty.' It is subtler: the restlessness itself is evidence. You didn't invent the hunger. You didn't choose it. It came with you. Augustine says that hunger is not random — it is a compass pointing at something real. The question is what. Augustine says God. A secular philosopher says: maybe meaning, maybe connection, maybe something we haven't named yet. But you can't say the restlessness isn't real. That is Augustine's move: start with desire, not with proof. Let the hunger ask the question. The answer is a separate conversation." }],
        bigIdea: "Faith does not replace reason. Faith gives reason its starting point — the conviction that the desire for truth is not arbitrary.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Map the restlessness", minutes: 16, steps: ["Name one thing you wanted badly, got, and then found yourself wanting something else. What was the pattern?", "Augustine says the pattern is evidence — not proof — that what you're looking for is not in the things you get. Do you agree?", "What would 'proof' look like for this kind of claim? Is proof even the right standard?", "Practice/Reflect: is the restlessness a compass or just an appetite? How would you tell the difference?"] }],
