        parentBriefing: "One object. What we call evil. Read the short reading aloud. On paper, with no account, say the English answer before the name. The sentence to say is Shaw's: \"For what is that which we call evil but the absence of good?\" The same chapter says the wound or disease is not a substance. That picture stays inside this one claim. It is not a second lesson. The name privation comes only after that English. The parent holds whether the claim is true. Do not teach the darkness comparison. Do not replace it. Do not use this page as comfort for a child in pain. There is no account, no model prompt, and no Hermes card. Parent note: Not part of the reading aloud. The English is Professor J. F. Shaw's, in the Enchiridion, chapter 11, \"What is Called Evil in the Universe is But the Absence of Good.\" The sentence is: \"For what is that which we call evil but the absence of good?\" The Latin of that line, same chapter, is: \"Quid est autem aliud quod malum dicitur, nisi privatio boni?\" Shaw's \"absence of good\" is his English for *privatio boni*. The child's page does not ask anyone to learn the Latin. The one new name, after the English, is privation. The wound sentence, same chapter, still in Shaw's English: \"for the wound or disease is not a substance, but a defect in the fleshly substance,—the flesh itself being a substance, and therefore something good, of which those evils—that is, privations of the good which we call health—are accidents. Just in the same way, what are called vices in the soul are nothing but privations of natural good.\" The child's reading uses the wound, not the vices, so the sitting stays one picture. The word \"accidents\" stays in this note. It is not a second term on the child's page. The Latin of the wound, same place: \"non enim ulla substantia, sed carnalis substantiae vitium est vulnus aut morbus.\" He does not, in one clause of this chapter, write \"evil is the absence of good, not a substance.\" The compound is his argument, not one sentence of his. A second book says the \"not a substance\" half of evil itself. Confessions, Book VII, Chapter 12, section 18, in J. G. Pilkington's English: \"That evil, then, which I sought whence it was, is not any substance; for were it a substance, it would be good.\" The same paragraph says \"all which is corrupted is deprived of good.\" It does not use the phrase \"absence of good.\" The footnote on that page — \"Evil, with Augustin, is a 'privation of good'\" — is Pilkington's note, not Augustine's sentence. Do not teach it as his words. Do not move the Confessions sentence into the child's reading as if it were the Enchiridion line.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not teach \"like darkness is the absence of light.\" That comparison is not in the chapters opened. Do not replace it with another comparison.", "Do not say he wrote \"evil is the absence of good, not a substance\" as one sentence. The absence sentence is Shaw's English for this chapter. In this chapter, \"not a substance\" is said of the wound or disease. The sentence that evil itself \"is not any substance\" is a different book. The parent may know that. The child does not recite it as this chapter.", "Do not use this page as comfort for a child in pain. \"It is just the absence of good\" is not comfort.", "Do not add the will, a lesser good, moral evil and natural evil, an earthquake, or genocide. Those are not this sitting.", "The parent holds the question. Do not ask a model. No account."],
