        dek: "Good is that which all things seek after. That is the first principle of practical reason. Good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided. That is the first precept of law, not the first principle. The parent holds the distinction.",
        objective: "Say those two English sentences in that order. The first is the first principle of practical reason. The second is the first precept of law. Do not drop \"and pursued.\"",
        parentBriefing: "One object. Those two sentences, and which name belongs to which. Read the short reading aloud. On paper, with no account, the family writes both sentences and does not shorten the second. English first. The names come after the sentences they name. The wording is Question 94, Article 2, as the note shows. Do not teach \"human beings can, by reason alone, discover moral principles embedded in human nature.\" Do not replace that sentence. Do not call \"good is to be done and evil avoided\" the first principle. Do not drop \"and pursued.\" Do not say the shortened sentence is self-evident, and do not ask whether it is circular. The parent holds the distinction. There is no account, no model prompt, and no Hermes card. Parent note: Not part of the reading aloud. The English is the Dominican translation of Prima Secundae, Question 94, Article 2, in the body of the answer. The child's page says the quoted words first, then the name. His sentence is longer than that label. Same body: \"Consequently the first principle of practical reason is one founded on the notion of good, viz. that \"good is that which all things seek after.\" Hence this is the first precept of law, that \"good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided.\" All other precepts of the natural law are based upon this: so that whatever the practical reason naturally apprehends as man's good (or evil) belongs to the precepts of the natural law as something to be done or avoided.\" \"One founded on the notion of good\" stays in this note. It is the same principle, not a second one. The clause about all other precepts stays in this note. It is not a second line the child says. It is not a replacement for the sentence this page does not teach. He does not call \"good is to be done and evil avoided\" the first principle. The first-precept sentence includes \"and pursued.\" The old page dropped those words. Earlier in the same body: \"the precepts of the natural law are to the practical reason, what the first principles of demonstrations are to the speculative reason; because both are self-evident principles.\" Self-evident is said of the precepts as a class. The same paragraph splits self-evident in itself from self-evident to us, and propositions self-evident to all from propositions self-evident only to the wise. It does not use the sentence \"The first principle is self-evident: good is to be done and evil avoided.\" This page does not teach that class, and it does not teach the split. The child does not recite either. Latin of the same article, paragraph [37592]: \"Et ideo primum principium in ratione practica est quod fundatur supra rationem boni, quae est, bonum est quod omnia appetunt. Hoc est ergo primum praeceptum legis, quod bonum est faciendum et prosequendum, et malum vitandum.\" The first-precept sentence includes *et prosequendum*. The child's page does not ask anyone to learn the Latin. Earlier in the same paragraph: \"praecepta legis naturae hoc modo se habent ad rationem practicam, sicut principia prima demonstrationum se habent ad rationem speculativam, utraque enim sunt quaedam principia per se nota.\" *Per se nota* is said of both sets of principles. It is not said of the old page's shortened sentence. The sentence \"human beings can, by reason alone, discover moral principles embedded in human nature\" is not in this reading. It is not replaced. The check is under One claim.",
        hardEdges: ["Do not teach \"human beings can, by reason alone, discover moral principles embedded in human nature.\" Questions 94 and 91, in the English opened for the brief, do not mention that sentence. Do not replace it with another claim.", "Do not call \"good is to be done and evil avoided\" the first principle. Do not drop \"and pursued.\" The first principle of practical reason is \"good is that which all things seek after.\" The first precept of law is \"good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided.\"", "Do not say the shortened sentence is self-evident. In that article, self-evident is said of the precepts as a class, not of the page's shortened sentence. Do not ask whether it is self-evident or circular.", "The parent holds the distinction. Do not ask a model. No account."],
        reading: [{ heading: "Two sentences", body: "Good is that which all things seek after. Say that first. That is the first principle of practical reason. Then the next sentence, and do not shorten it: good is to be done and pursued, and evil is to be avoided. That is the first precept of law. It is not the first principle. The parent holds the distinction." }],
