        parentBriefing: "The objective is that sentence, and a parent can say it with no Greek in it. Say it before any Greek word. Then one historical fact only: the Nicene letter is not Basil of Caesarea's formula. Do not say the Cappadocians argued about a preposition. A letter is not a preposition. The parent holds any question past the sentence. There is no model prompt. Parent aside, after the English sentence has been said: homoousios means same essence; homoiousios means similar essence; the extra mark is the letter iota. Ousia is the one essence. Hypostasis is each particular who. None of those words is a preposition. Parent note, not part of the student reading: Eusebius of Caesarea, writing home after Nicaea, says the council added one word, that the Son is one in essence with the Father. Basil of Caesarea, Letter 236, section 6, is a different argument: one essence, and the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit not blurred into one. Basil of Caesarea is not Basil of Ancyra. The letter is not dated here. No Greek creed line is quoted.",
